(window.LADDER_CARDS["28a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["28a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"28a1.1","q":"With several inputs, when is a point x near w, and what does the each-place technique say?","a":"when the distance |x − w| is small, which makes every place close at once; each place xᵢ is a continuous function of the point, and so is every sum, product and quotient of continuous functions where the bottom is not 0","w":["when one of its places is close to that of w; each place is continuous but products of places need not be","when x lies on a line through w; a function is continuous when it is continuous along every such line","when every place is equal to that of w; only straight maps are continuous"],"why":"|xᵢ − wᵢ| ≤ |x − w|."},
 {"u":"28a2.1","q":"What is a partial slope, and what does the one-knob technique say?","a":"the slope of f as one input moves and the others are held still; to find it, treat every other input as a fixed number and use the slope techniques of rung 13","w":["the slope of f along the line y = x; found by adding the slopes of all the inputs","a part of the slope of f; found by dividing the slope by the number of inputs","the slope of f as every input moves at once; found by the product technique on all of them"],"why":"For x²y + y³: f_x = 2xy and f_y = x² + 3y²."},
 {"u":"28a3.1","q":"What does the knobs-miss-jumps technique say, and why can both knobs miss the jump?","a":"a function can have both partial slopes at a point and still not be continuous there; the two knobs look only along two lines through the point, and the jump can hide on every other line","w":["a function with both partial slopes is continuous; the knobs see every direction between them","a function that is continuous has both partial slopes; the knobs miss only corners","a function with one partial slope has the other; a jump shows on both knobs"],"why":"So a slope that speaks for every direction must be more than a list of partial slopes."},
 {"u":"28a4.1","q":"What is wrong with “f heads to L along every line through w, so f heads to L at w”?","a":"near means every road in, curved ones too; a function can head to L along every line and to something else along a curved road, as x²y/(x⁴ + y²) does along y = x²","w":["nothing; the lines through w cover every point near w","it fails only when f has no value at w","it holds once the limit along the two axes agree"],"why":"A curved road can still find a step."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["28b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["28b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"28b1.1","q":"With several inputs and outputs, what does it mean for f to have a slope at w, and what does the one-flat-map technique say?","a":"there is a straight map L, the best flat map, with f(w + h) = f(w) + L(h) + r(h) where |r(h)|/|h| heads to 0; and a function has at most one best flat map at a point","w":["every partial slope exists at w; and there may be many flat maps","there is a straight map L with |r(h)| bounded; and L is unique","f is continuous at w; and its flat map is the zero map"],"why":"The left-over must shrink faster than the step."},
 {"u":"28b2.1","q":"What does the slope-grid technique say, and why does a best flat map make f continuous at w?","a":"with a best flat map L at w, every partial slope of every output exists and the grid of L has ∂fᵢ/∂xⱼ(w) in row i, column j; and f(w + h) − f(w) = L(h) + r(h), where both terms head to 0 with h","w":["the grid of L has ∂fⱼ/∂xᵢ in row i, column j; f is continuous because its partial slopes exist","the grid of L holds the values fᵢ(w); continuity is not implied","the grid of L is the second-slope grid; f is continuous because L is bounded"],"why":"The slope along a unit u is L(u): one multiplication."},
 {"u":"28b3.1","q":"What does the smooth-knobs technique say, how does its proof walk from (w, b) to (w + h, b + k), and what is a smoothly sloped function?","a":"if every partial slope exists near w and is continuous at w, f has a best flat map there; the proof walks one knob at a time, along x and then along y, using the average-slope technique on each leg; a smoothly sloped function is one whose partial slopes all exist and are continuous","w":["if every partial slope exists at w, f has a best flat map; the proof walks straight along the diagonal; smoothly sloped means continuous","if f has a best flat map, its partial slopes are continuous; the proof walks backwards; smoothly sloped means having second slopes","if one partial slope is continuous, f has a best flat map; the proof uses one leg; smoothly sloped means sloped along one input"],"why":"Formulas built from places are smoothly sloped wherever their bottoms are not 0."},
 {"u":"28b4.1","q":"What is wrong with “|r(h)|/|h| bounded, so L is the best flat map”?","a":"the left-over over the step must head to 0, not just stay small; bounded would let many straight maps pass","w":["the ratio must head to 1","nothing; bounded is what best means","the ratio must be 0 for every h"],"why":"Only heading to 0 makes the flat map unique."});
